1 NOTE <p>DF</p><p><p>Notes for DANIEL (DONIE SEOIRSE) FRIEL:</p></p><p><p></p></p><p><p>Daniel Friel's  name continues to exist in Fanad in  2000 in the reference to the farm and house in Ballymichael as "D
2 CONC onie's". He is recorded as "Daniel Freil" in the 1901 Census, He died on 31 Dec 1903 in Ballymichael, based on his death record as reviewed in the registrars office in Letterkenny in Aug 2005. His dea
2 CONC th was notified by Art, his son. He gives his age as 65 in the 1901 Census, hence the assumption re his date of birth. His age is also noted as 68 on his death record, which gives chronic diphteria as
2 CONC  the cause of death. The 1901 Census notes him as a farmer, living in Ballymichael, states that he can read and write, and speaks both Irish and English. The locations given for his birth is on the as
2 CONC sumption that he was born in the location (Ballymichael) in which his father (George Seoirse Friel) was recorded as living in 1834 - as "George Freel" -  in the Tithes Applotments, and that he also di
2 CONC ed at this location.</p><p><p>Big Paddy Callaghan relates how Donie and his brother Michael at one stage agreed to split the farm between them. This was contrary to the wishes of the landlord, Lord Le
2 CONC itrim who evicted them both. Donie went to stay with relatives in Rosnakill ( and some of the family are noted as being born in Rosnakill in the late 1870s ), but was subsequently allowed to return to
2 CONC  the farm. Paddy also suggested that Donie may have spent some time in Lifford Gaol as a result of a clash with Lord Leitrim - he may have been convicted of stealing turf.  Eileen Carragher tells that
2 CONC  Donie undertook the drainage of the big field on the left of the lane from the road to Donie's house in Ballymichael. His brother, Michael went to Cashel near Portsalon, when his descendants still li
2 CONC ve. The date of his marriage was obtained from the LDS site.</p><p><p>t</p></p><p></p>
